From: nuubOS Project <noreply@nuubos.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] allwinner: h616: add native PSCI system suspend

H616/H700 have no AR100 management core, so there is no SCP firmware
(crust) to implement SYSTEM_SUSPEND and Linux could only use s2idle.

Implement it natively on CPU 0: for a system-level power-down the core
stays powered and the GIC CPU interface stays on. pwr_domain_pwr_down
moves CPU 0 to OSC24M, stops PLL_CPUX, lowers VDD-CPU (AXP717 DCDC1 over
R_I2C, whose Linux-owned setup is saved around each access) to 0.81 V,
copies a small position-independent routine to SRAM A1 (unused by
Linux), turns the MMU off and runs it. The routine stops the DRAM
masters, puts the DRAM in software self-refresh, moves AHB/APB to
OSC24M, stops every PLL but PLL_DDR and waits in WFI with IRQ/FIQ routed
to EL3 (Linux masked every non-wake interrupt). On wake everything is
restored in reverse order and BL31 re-enters through
bl31_warm_entrypoint, as Cortex-A53 cannot abandon a power-down.

VDD-SYS stays on, so device state and wake sources are the same as in
s2idle. The DRAM controller clock must keep running: with CLKEN cleared
it never leaves self-refresh again. RTC general purpose register 6 holds
the last standby step (0x5a5a000N, 0x5a5a00e1/e6 self-refresh entry/exit
timeout, GP5 then holds STATR), GP5 the VDD-CPU result (0x5a5b00NN, the
DCDC1 register read before lowering it).

The self-refresh sequence follows Crust's H6 DRAM driver
(drivers/dram/sun50i-h6-dram.c, BSD-3-Clause OR GPL-2.0-only).

Signed-off-by: nuubOS Project <noreply@nuubos.invalid>
---
